 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "URL rules are queued at definition time and applied to the app at registration time.\n\n1. **Queue the rule.** `Bundle.attach_route_rule` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:413`) is also what the route decorators reach. It validates the endpoint and view names, then calls `self.record(lambda s: s.attach_route_rule(...))`. `Bundle.record` (`bundles.py:224`) appends the lambda to `self.deferred_functions`.\n\n2. **Register the bundle.** `App.register_bundle` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:571`) just calls `bundle.register(app, options)`.\n\n3. **Replay the queue.** `Bundle.register` (`bundles.py:273`) does the following:\n   - Records the bundle in `app.bundles` under its name.\n   - Builds a `BundleSetupState` via `make_setup_state` (`bundles.py:246`).\n   - Adds the static-file route through `state.attach_route_rule` if the bundle has a static folder.\n   - Loops `for deferred in self.deferred_functions: deferred(state)` (`bundles.py:334`).\n\n4. **Apply the rule to the app.** `BundleSetupState.attach_route_rule` (`bundles.py:87`) does the following:\n   - Prepends `url_prefix` to the rule.\n   - Defaults the subdomain.\n   - Merges `url_defaults`.\n   - Prefixes the endpoint as `{name_prefix}.{name}.{endpoint}`.\n   - Calls `self.app.attach_route_rule(...)` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:606`), which actually adds the rule to the app.\n\n`BundleSetupState.__init__` (`bundles.py:41`) is where the `url_prefix`, `subdomain` and `url_defaults` overrides from the registration options are resolved.",
